---
import { existsSync } from 'node:fs';
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import MarginNote from '../components/MarginNote.astro';

// Headshot slot: reserved, not required. Drop a file at public/about/headshot.{webp,jpg,jpeg,png}
// and rebuild — it appears framed below, plain, no code change needed. Absent, this renders
// nothing; there is no broken-image state either way. See src/content/README.md and the root
// README's "Adding content" section for the convention.
const headshotExts = ['webp', 'jpg', 'jpeg', 'png'];
const headshotExt = headshotExts.find((ext) =>
  existsSync(fileURLToPath(new URL(`../../public/about/headshot.${ext}`, import.meta.url))),
);
const headshotSrc = headshotExt ? `/about/headshot.${headshotExt}` : null;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Security and compliance analyst — PCI DSS, SOC 2, ISO 27001, and the gap between what a control says and what the system does."
>
  <div itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Person">
    <!-- Microdata, not JSON-LD: the site's CSP forbids inline <script> without a hash, so
         entity facts for Google/AI crawlers live as itemprop attributes on real HTML
         instead of a script block. Values mirror the visible text, not invented ones. -->
    <meta itemprop="name" content="Joe Carpenter" />
    <meta itemprop="alternateName" content="JP Carpenter" />
    <meta itemprop="jobTitle" content="Security and Compliance Analyst" />
    <meta itemprop="email" content="joe@jpcarpenter.com" />
    <meta itemprop="url" content="https://jpcarpenter.com/about" />
  <section class="band sheet">
    <MarginNote index="04" stamp="About" note="Origin · work · conviction" />
    <div>
      <h1 class="band-heading">About</h1>
      {
        headshotSrc && (
          <figure class="about-photo">
            <img src={headshotSrc} alt="Joe Carpenter" width="220" height="220" loading="eager" />
          </figure>
        )
      }
      <p class="band-lede">
        I close the gap between what an organization says it controls and what the systems
        actually do — policies, assessments, and certifications that have to survive an auditor.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band sheet">
    <MarginNote stamp="Before" note="HIPAA · education" />
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Where the habit started</h2>
      <p>
        I started as a case manager working with HIPAA-regulated patient records. That is where
        compliance stopped being a checklist — I was accountable when the documentation did not
        match what actually happened.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then thirteen years as an educator in Taiwan. That job was teaching and school life, not
        IT. I did some informal tech work and helped during the COVID remote stretch, but I was
        not running the network. What carried forward was institutional discipline: records,
        process, and being answerable when something was wrong.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band sheet">
    <MarginNote stamp="Now" note="briskData · since Feb 2026" state="verified" />
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What I actually do</h2>
      <p>
        I currently work with briskData, as a contractor, under the title Compliance Manager.
        Recent work includes leading a PCI DSS certification effort, migrating the compliance
        program from Vanta to Scrut, and driving SOC 2 and ISO 27001 toward certification — with
        ISO 42001 also in scope. Alongside the frameworks: security policy writing, and the
        security training used in onboarding.
      </p>
      <p>
        The write-ups in <a href="/labs">Labs</a> are how I keep the technical side sharp.
        Unfinished work is labeled that way in the margin rather than dressed up as done.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band sheet">
    <MarginNote stamp="Conviction" state="verified" />
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What I think the job actually is</h2>
      <p>
        Most compliance failures are not failures of knowledge. Everyone involved could recite
        the requirement. The failure is that nobody read closely enough to notice the document
        and the system had stopped agreeing with each other.
      </p>
      <p>
        A vendor answering "yes, encrypted at rest" has told you a sentence, not a control. A
        policy that nobody onboards against is a filing, not a program. The work is closing that
        gap, out loud, in writing somebody else can check. That is the whole reason the phrase
        on the front page is <em>read what you sign</em>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band sheet">
    <MarginNote stamp="How" note="What that means in practice" />
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>How I show up</h2>
      <p>
        I read the control, the vendor answer, and the configuration until they agree — or I
        write down where they don't. I am useful on teams that want a second reader on compliance
        and security work: someone who will own the evidence trail, not paper over the gap.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band sheet">
    <MarginNote stamp="Credentials" note="After the work, not before" />
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Certifications</h2>
      <p>
        CompTIA Security+ (DoD 8570/8140 compliant), Network+, A+, CSIS (Secure Infrastructure
        Specialist), and CIOS (IT Operations Specialist).
      </p>
      <p>
        In progress, and labeled that way until they are not: BTL1 (Blue Team Level 1) and AWS
        Cloud Practitioner.
      </p>
      <p>Bachelor of Arts, History and Chinese Language — United States.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="closing">
    <div class="sheet closing-inner">
      <MarginNote stamp="Open" state="open" note="Plain email · no form" />
      <div>
        <h2>Reach me</h2>
        <p>
          Plain email is best: <a href="mailto:joe@jpcarpenter.com">joe@jpcarpenter.com</a>. No
          contact form, because forms punish humans and catch bots anyway. Code and lab notes
          live at <a href="https://github.com/joepcarpenter" rel="me" itemprop="sameAs">github.com/joepcarpenter</a>.
        </p>
        <div class="desk-actions">
          <a class="btn-primary" href="/labs">Read the evidence</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Plain frame, no circle, no glow ring — same hairline treatment as the Labs evidence
     figures (global.css .lab-detail-body figure), not a portrait-hero look. */
  .about-photo {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    max-width: 12rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.75rem;
  }

  .about-photo img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  .prose {
    max-width: 38rem;
  }

  .prose h2 {
    font-size: 1.3rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.7rem;
  }

  .prose p {
    color: var(--ink-body);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .prose em {
    font-style: normal;
    color: var(--accent-soft);
  }
</style>
